1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Birthdate listed is incorrect baptismal date'''
2 CONT MRS. H. H. LEE
2 CONT LAID TO REST
2 CONT Came to America Alone From Bergen, Norway, as a Girl of Sixteen Years
2 CONT Settled on Farm Near Britt in 1877 and Had Spent Balance of Life Here
2 CONT Another long time resident and fine Christian character has turned her earthly labors over to others and has made the great adventure into the spirit world. Funeral services for her were conducted at the home at 1:30 and at 2 o'cl
2 CONC ock at the Methodist church Tuesday allernoon, her pastor, the Rev. R. Berntsen, assisted by the Rev. G. W. Eggleston, in charge. Mrs. O. N. Siverson of Badger, sang a solo and the choir sang favorite sacred hymns. Sorrowing relat
2 CONC ives and friends followed her body to the grave and saw it buried in Evergreen cemetery in a grave beside that of her husbend who died May 29, 1895.
2 CONT Anna Larson was born at Bergen, Norway, Sept. 29, 1850, and died at Britt, lowa, at 3:30 p. m. Saturday, April 28, 1934, aged 83 years, 6 months and 29 days.
2 CONT When a girl she came to America alone in 1866 and settled at Deerfield, Wis. She was a tailoress and worked at Deerfield, Madison and Chiengo On July 18, 1869, she united in marriage with H. H. Lee at Deerfield, Wis. For eight yea
2 CONC rs they lived at Deerfield and then came west to the frontier, settling on a farm two miles northeast at Britt in 1877, where they lived and prospered for many years.
2 CONT In 1913 Mrs. Lee left the farm and came to Britt and purchased a home and there spent her remaining years in happy companionship with her daughter Sara and son Hilly.
2 CONT Mrs. Lee was a very active woman, interested in the best things in life.
2 CONT She was a charter member of the Evangelical Free church at Britt and was the last charter member to pass this life She remained active in the work of the church until death, a faithful life service that will not soon be forgotten 
2 CONC by members of that church.
2 CONT As a mother Mrs. Lee had no superiors; as a neighbor and friend she was ever faithful, kind and considerate. In all her associations she made friends and kept them. I while her life was dedicated to her family and her church, she 
2 CONC found time to work in good causes, those with uplifting influence. She was a lover of nature Mrs. Lee's wonderful flower gardens carried good cheer and magnified her fine character. About the home yard as she died, precious flower
2 CONC s that she had cultivated and cared for were sending forth their first green shoots to brighten up the landscape as the life of Mrs. Lee had done. And it is a pleasant thought to the family that just as her favorite flowers were s
2 CONC tarting forth for another term of new and lovely life, so has their mother gone forth to a more glorious and beautiful life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving children are Mis. B. J. Johansen of Minneapolis, 
2 CONT Mrs, L. 0. Myrland of Badger, Iowa; 
2 CONT Sherman Lee of Port Huron, Mich.; 
2 CONT H.M. Lee and Miss Sara Lee, of Britt.
2 CONT There are seven grandchildren and five great grandchildren.
