1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Vivian Eixenberger Obituary
2 CONT
2 CONT Vivian M. Tamisiea Eixenberger "We love you, Mom"  Vivian M. Tamisiea Eixenberger passed away at home on March 20, 2003 surrounded by her loved ones from complications of pulmonary  fibrosis. Vivian was born at home in Missouri Va
2 CONC lley, Iowa on November 30, 1923 to Joseph Earl Tamisiea and Myrtle V. Tamisiea. Vivian spent her early years growing up in Missouri Valley, where her father owned a small farm supply and hardware store. She was very proud of her m
2 CONC idwestern roots and family. She was the oldest of four sisters, with whom she remained close throughout her life. After graduation from high school, Vivian attended the University of Iowa where she received a B.A. degree in Educat
2 CONC ion. After graduation, she moved to Rapid City, South Dakota to accept a teaching job, where she met her future husband. On May 23, 1951, Vivian married Bernard D. Eixenberger. They enjoyed 48 years of marriage together, and were 
2 CONC the proud parents of six children. As they shared their life together, military duties and job opportunities led them to live in many parts of the country, including: Rapid City, SD, Aberdeen, MD, Sedalia, MO, Seattle, WA, Denver,
2 CONC  CO, and Topeka KS before settling in Salt Lake City. Vivian's calling in life was her family. She was the focal point of unconditional love to her husband, children, and grandchildren. She will be greatly missed for her gentle pe
2 CONC rsonality, resourcefulness, kindness, patience, consideration of others, and love to all who knew her. Vivian enjoyed sewing, reading, spending time at Bear Lake, talking non stop with sisters, playing games with her grandchildren
2 CONC  and simply listening to the daily activities of her family. Vivian was preceded in death by her husband, parents and infant son, Joseph. She is survived by her three sisters: Barbara Purtle of Omaha, Nebraska, Maurine (Chuck) Gri
2 CONC ffith of Ann Arbor, Michigan, and Carol Haines of Denver, Colorado, sister-in-law Mary (Dell) Hellekson of Rapid City, SD; five children: Jim (Norine) Eixenberger of Phoenix, Arizona, Jean (Dave) Knaus of Magna, Utah, Bob (Lisa) E
2 CONC ixenberger of Sandy, Utah, Ken (Francene) Eixenberger of Boise, Idaho, David (Susan) Eixenberger of Sandy, Utah; thirteen grandchildren; numerous nieces, nephews, and cousins. Funeral services (Mass of Christian Burial) will be he
2 CONC ld at 12:00 noon on Monday, March 24th at St. Francis Xavier Catholic Church, 4501 West 5215 South in Kearns, Utah. Friends and family may visit from 5-6:30 p.m. on Sunday evening, March 23rd at Wasatch Lawn Mortuary, 3401 South H
2 CONC ighland Drive. Interment, Wasatch Lawn Memorial Park. The family would like to thank Hospice Caresource and Bayada Nursing for their loving care during Vivian's illness.
