1 NAME Robert Craig /Eixenberger/
2 GIVN Robert Craig
2 SURN Eixenberger
2 _MARNM Eixenberger
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Mount Olivet Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Salt Lake City
3 STAE Utah
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 APR 1956
2 ADDR
3 CITY Denver
3 STAE CO
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 JAN 2017
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Find A Grave
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/175593986/robert-craig-eixenberger
2 CONT
2 CONT Surrounded by loved ones, Robert Craig Eixenberger (Bob) passed away on January 17, 2017 from complications of Pulmonary Fibrosis. He was 60 years young. Bob was an exceptional father, husband, son, brother, friend, co- worker, an
2 CONC d a fair golfer. He was born on April 12th, 1956 in Denver, Colorado to Bernard Dale Eixenberger and Vivian Tamisiea Eixenberger. He was part of a large and loving family including four brothers, one sister, numerous in-laws, niec
2 CONC es, nephews, cousins, aunts and uncles. He remained close to his family throughout his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of all, Bob loved his family and sel essly gave of himself. Lisa and Nick were the center of his universe, and he was most happy watching them have fun. Even though he was faced with a challenging disease, he fought with dete
2 CONC rmination to live another day for his family. Bob was heartbroken with the loss of his infant son Andrew, but cherished the few short days he had with him. He was extremely proud of his son Nick and was looking forward to seeing h
2 CONC im graduate this year from the U, and his goal to continue his education in the medical eld. Some of Bob's favorite times with Nick were watching him in the MUSS student section at Utah games, skiing, playing golf, vacations, oat 
2 CONC trips, or simply helping his dad at the cabin.
2 CONT
2 CONT His early years were spent in Denver, Colorado and Rapid City, South Dakota, prior to moving to Salt Lake City around age 10. During this time he developed a passion for music, art, golf, skiing, photography, and many other activi
2 CONC ties that he would continue to enjoy throughout his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an outstanding and creative student graduating with honors from Cyprus High School, where he played football and was in the band. As his education continued, he graduated Magna Cum Laude from the University of Utah with a B
2 CONC achelors in Chemical Engineering and a Masters in Business Administration. He was also a member of the Tau Beta Pi Honor society.
2 CONT
2 CONT No longer looking down at the textbooks, Bob met and married the love of his life, Lisa Bernhisel on October 24, 1987 at the Cathedral of the Madeline in Salt Lake City, Utah. Together, they had two sons, Andrew and Nicholas, and 
2 CONC celebrated nearly 30 years of marriage together.
2 CONT
2 CONT With Lisa as his rudder, Bob began a lifelong career working in the carbon- ber/aerospace industry. During this time, he continued to challenge himself to grow personally and professionally. This led him to many new opportunities,
2 CONC  relocating to Delaware, Alabama, and California, with the excitement of occasional travel to Europe and Asia on business. Together, Bob and Lisa embraced each day as an opportunity to meet new friends, explore new surroundings, e
2 CONC xperience great food, and lose a few more golf balls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beginning as a Process Engineer with Hercules Incorporated, Bob quickly advanced in his career to hold several key leadership positions with the Hexcel Corporation including Director of Sales and Marketing (Newark Delaware), Plant
2 CONC  Manager (Decatur, AL), Operations Manager (Bacchus, UT), Vice President and General Manager for GKN Aerospace (Montgomery, AL), Business Director for Cytec Engineered Materials (Anaheim, CA ). Ultimately, Bob returned to Utah whe
2 CONC re he worked as a Senior Program Manager for Commercial Aircraft with Orbital ATK until the time of his passing. When talking about work, he said his greatest moments were the numerous friendships he developed over his career.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Bob was best known for his inquisitive, creative and caring personality which transcended all aspects of his life. He always treated others with kindness and genuinely held an interest in the lives of others. He treated t
2 CONC he janitor with the same respect as the CEO. He was loyal, true to his values and held himself to a high standard of ethics. He loved his dogs, dressing up for Halloween and cheering for the Utah Utes. Bob truly embraced and lived
2 CONC  life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bob was also known for his analytical problem solving skills and attention to detail. This could make ordering ice cream a dif cult process, but also helped him to work on a project until nished to perfection, creating incredible 
2 CONC art work, building a cabin, or learning to ski.
2 CONT
2 CONT He enjoyed the company of his immediate and extended family, and continuously mentored his son, many nieces and nephews with words of encouragement and a challenge to explore life.
2 CONT
2 CONT They say life is a test of one's character, and once again Bob, you nished top of the class. Until we meet again, we love you and will miss you.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bob is survived by his wife Lisa Eixenberger; son Nicholas H. Eixenberger; brothers James (Norine) Eixenberger of Phoenix, AZ, Kenneth (Francene) Eixenberger of Boise, Idaho, David (Susan) Eixenberger, of Sandy, Utah; sister Jean 
2 CONC (Dave) Knaus of Magna, Utah; numerous aunts, uncles, cousins, nieces, and nephews; and cherished in-laws (Bernhisel family). Preceded in death by his parents, Bernard and Vivian Eixenberger, his infant son Andrew Eixenberger, and 
2 CONC infant brother Joseph Eixenberger.
